            <ArticleTitle>What Are the Professional, Political, and Ethical Challenges of Co-Creating Health Care Systems?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1132-1138</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">journalofethics.2017.19.11.msoc1-1711</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/journalofethics.2017.19.11.msoc1-1711</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Co-creation is seen by many as a means of meeting the multiple challenges facing contemporary health care systems by involving institutions, professionals, patients, and stakeholders in new roles, relationships, and collaborative practices. While co-creation has the potential to positively transform health care systems, it generates a number of political and ethical challenges that should not be overlooked. We suggest that those involved in envisioning and implementing co-creation initiatives pay close attention to significant questions of equity, power, and justice and to the fundamental challenge of securing a common vision of the aims of and agendas for health care systems. While such initiatives present significant opportunities for improvement, they need to be viewed in light of their accompanying professional, political, and ethical challenges.</AbstractText>
